/**
 * @page concepts Kernel Concepts
 * @brief ChibiOS Kernel Concepts
 * This page describes the concepts shared by the ChibiOS/RT and ChibiOS/NIL
 * kernels. The API classes and the system states also apply to ChibiOS/HAL
 * and XHAL, whose functions are documented with the same classes. Where the
 * two kernels differ the difference is stated, NIL is functionally a subset
 * of RT.
 *
 * - @ref naming
 * - @ref api_suffixes
 * - @ref system_states
 * - @ref interrupt_classes
 * - @ref thread_concepts
 * - @ref time_concepts
 * - @ref object_concepts
 * - @ref instance_concepts
 * - @ref error_detection
 * .
 *
 * @section naming Naming Conventions
 * The kernel APIs are named following the convention
 * <tt>ch\<Group\>\<Action\>\<Suffix\>()</tt>, the group identifies the module
 * and the suffix identifies the API class, see @ref api_suffixes. For example
 * chSemWaitTimeoutS() is the @a WaitTimeout action of the @a Sem module,
 * an S-Class API.<br>
 * Groups of the kernel modules:
 * - @a Sys, system.
 * - @a Sch, scheduler.
 * - @a VT, virtual timers and system time.
 * - @a Thd, threads.
 * - @a Sem, @a Mtx, @a Cond, @a Evt, @a Msg, semaphores, mutexes,
 *   condition variables, events and messages.
 * - @a Reg, registry.
 * - @a TM, time measurement.
 * - @a Dbg, @a Sft, @a RFCU, debug, safety and runtime faults.
 * .
 * Groups of the OS library modules: @a BSem binary semaphores, @a MB
 * mailboxes, @a Pipe pipes, @a Core, @a Heap, @a Pool and @a GuardedPool
 * memory allocators, @a Fifo objects FIFOs, @a Factory objects factory,
 * @a Delegate delegate threads and @a Job jobs queues.<br>
 * Other naming rules:
 * - Types are lowercase with a @p _t suffix, for example @p thread_t,
 *   @p semaphore_t, @p msg_t, @p systime_t.
 * - Kernel configuration options in @p chconf.h are named @p CH_CFG_xxx,
 *   debug options @p CH_DBG_xxx.
 * - Port layer definitions are named @p PORT_xxx.
 * - Static initializers of objects are named @p xxx_DECL(), see
 *   @ref object_concepts.
 * .
 *
 * @section api_suffixes API Classes
 * Each API belongs to a class stating the system states it can be invoked
 * from, see @ref system_states. The class is encoded in the API name suffix:
 * - <b>No suffix</b>, normal APIs can be invoked only from thread context,
 *   in the <b>Normal</b> state, outside of any lock zone and not from
 *   interrupt handlers. Examples: chSemWait(), chThdSleep().
 * - @anchor I-Class <b>"I"</b>, I-Class APIs can be invoked only from the
 *   <b>I-Locked</b> or <b>S-Locked</b> states, from a lock zone in a thread
 *   or in an interrupt handler. These functions never reschedule, invoked
 *   from a thread an explicit reschedule could be required before leaving
 *   the lock zone, for example using chSchRescheduleS().<br>
 *   Being in an interrupt handler is not enough: the handler must enter the
 *   I-Locked state using chSysLockFromISR() before invoking any I-Class API.
 *   Examples: chSemSignalI(), chEvtBroadcastFlagsI().
 * - @anchor S-Class <b>"S"</b>, S-Class APIs can be invoked only from the
 *   <b>S-Locked</b> state, from a lock zone in a thread. These functions can
 *   reschedule internally but are not guaranteed to do so, for example a
 *   wait on a semaphore reschedules only if the semaphore counter is not
 *   positive. Examples: chSemWaitS(), chSchRescheduleS().
 * - @anchor X-Class <b>"X"</b>, X-Class APIs can be invoked from any
 *   context, fast interrupt handlers included, with or without a lock. They
 *   cannot be invoked from non-maskable interrupt handlers. Examples:
 *   chVTGetSystemTimeX(), chThdGetSelfX().
 * .
 *
 * @subsection function_classes Function Classes
 * The documentation of each function states its class in a
 * <b>Function Class</b> paragraph, the classes beyond the four above are
 * used for functions that do not follow the general rules:
 * | Function class        | Meaning                                         |
 * |-----------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
 * | Normal API            | No suffix, see above.                           |
 * | I-Class, S-Class, X-Class | See above.                                  |
 * | Special function      | The requirements are stated in the notes of the function, for example chSysLock() and chSysHalt(). |
 * | Module initializer    | Invoked once during the system initialization, for example the HAL drivers initializers invoked by halInit(). |
 * | Object instance constructor | Initializes an object, see @ref object_concepts. |
 * | Object instance finalizer   | Disposes an object, see @ref object_concepts. |
 * | Interrupt handler     | Must not be invoked directly.                   |
 * | Not an API            | Internal function, not meant for applications.  |
 *
 * @subsection state_checker State Checker
 * When the option @p CH_DBG_SYSTEM_STATE_CHECK is enabled the kernel
 * tracks the lock and interrupt nesting of each core and verifies the call
 * protocol at runtime. A violation halts the system, see
 * @ref error_detection, with one of the following reasons:
 * | Reason | Detected violation                                              |
 * |--------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
 * | SV#1   | chSysDisable() invoked from a lock zone or an interrupt handler. |
 * | SV#2   | chSysSuspend() invoked from a lock zone or an interrupt handler. |
 * | SV#3   | chSysEnable() invoked from a lock zone or an interrupt handler.  |
 * | SV#4   | chSysLock() invoked from a lock zone or an interrupt handler.    |
 * | SV#5   | chSysUnlock() invoked outside a lock zone or from an interrupt handler. |
 * | SV#6   | chSysLockFromISR() invoked outside an interrupt handler or from a lock zone. |
 * | SV#7   | chSysUnlockFromISR() invoked outside an interrupt handler or outside a lock zone. |
 * | SV#8   | @p CH_IRQ_PROLOGUE() executed within a lock zone.               |
 * | SV#9   | @p CH_IRQ_EPILOGUE() executed within a lock zone or without a matching prologue. |
 * | SV#10  | An I-Class API invoked outside a lock zone.                      |
 * | SV#11  | An S-Class API invoked outside a lock zone or from an interrupt handler. |
 *
 * @section system_states System States
 * The system can be in one of the following logical states:
 * - <b>Init</b>. The state after a reset, the maskable interrupt sources
 *   are disabled. Only the initialization functions, halInit() and
 *   chSysInit(), can be invoked.
 * - <b>Normal</b>. All the interrupt sources are enabled, threads are
 *   running. Normal APIs and X-Class APIs can be invoked.
 * - <b>Suspended</b>. The regular interrupt sources are disabled, the fast
 *   interrupt sources are enabled. Only X-Class APIs and the state change
 *   functions chSysDisable() and chSysEnable() can be invoked.
 * - <b>Disabled</b>. Both the regular and the fast interrupt sources are
 *   disabled. Only X-Class APIs and the state change functions
 *   chSysSuspend() and chSysEnable() can be invoked.
 * - <b>Sleep</b>. Architecture-dependent low power mode entered by the idle
 *   thread while waiting for interrupts. After an interrupt is served the
 *   Normal state is restored and the scheduler can reschedule.
 * - <b>S-Locked</b>. A thread is in a lock zone, entered with chSysLock().
 *   The regular interrupt sources are disabled, the fast interrupt sources
 *   are enabled. S-Class, I-Class and X-Class APIs can be invoked.
 * - <b>I-Locked</b>. An interrupt handler is in a lock zone, entered with
 *   chSysLockFromISR(). I-Class and X-Class APIs can be invoked.
 * - <b>Serving Regular Interrupt</b> (SRI). An interrupt handler of the
 *   regular class is executing outside a lock zone. Only X-Class APIs can
 *   be invoked, the handler enters the I-Locked state using
 *   chSysLockFromISR() in order to invoke I-Class APIs. Interrupt handlers
 *   can be preempted by higher priority interrupts on some architectures.
 * - <b>Serving Fast Interrupt</b> (SFI). Only X-Class APIs can be invoked.
 * - <b>Serving Non-Maskable Interrupt</b> (SNMI). No APIs can be invoked.
 * - <b>Halted</b>. All the interrupt sources are disabled and the system is
 *   stopped. This state is reached when an error is detected or after
 *   invoking chSysHalt(), see @ref error_detection.
 * .
 * The above states are logical states, they may have no associated
 * machine state on some architectures. Code that does not know the state it
 * runs in can use chSysGetStatusAndLockX() and chSysRestoreStatusX() in
 * order to enter and leave a lock zone, on ports supporting them.<br>
 * On multi-core systems each core has its own state. With
 * @p CH_CFG_SMP_MODE enabled the lock zone also excludes the other cores,
 * see @ref instance_concepts. The following diagram shows the transitions
 * between the states:
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.75", height="0.75"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    init [label="Init", style="bold"];
    norm [label="Normal", shape=doublecircle];
    susp [label="Suspended"];
    disab [label="Disabled"];
    slock [label="S-Locked"];
    ilock [label="I-Locked"];
    sleep [label="Sleep"];
    sri [label="SRI"];
    init -> norm [label="chSysInit()"];
    norm -> slock [label="chSysLock()", constraint=false];
    slock -> norm [label="chSysUnlock()"];
    norm -> susp [label="chSysSuspend()"];
    susp -> disab [label="chSysDisable()"];
    norm -> disab [label="chSysDisable()"];
    susp -> norm [label="chSysEnable()"];
    disab -> norm [label="chSysEnable()"];
    disab -> susp [label="chSysSuspend()"];
    slock -> ilock [label="Context Switch", dir="both"];
    norm -> sri [label="Regular IRQ", style="dotted"];
    sri -> norm [label="Regular IRQ return"];
    sri -> ilock [label="chSysLockFromISR()", constraint=false];
    ilock -> sri [label="chSysUnlockFromISR()"];
    norm -> sleep [label="Idle Thread"];
    sleep -> sri [label="Regular IRQ", style="dotted"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.75", height="0.75"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    init [label="Init", style="bold"];
    norm [label="Normal", shape=doublecircle];
    susp [label="Suspended"];
    disab [label="Disabled"];
    slock [label="S-Locked"];
    ilock [label="I-Locked"];
    sleep [label="Sleep"];
    sri [label="SRI"];
    init -> norm [label="chSysInit()"];
    norm -> slock [label="chSysLock()", constraint=false];
    slock -> norm [label="chSysUnlock()"];
    norm -> susp [label="chSysSuspend()"];
    susp -> disab [label="chSysDisable()"];
    norm -> disab [label="chSysDisable()"];
    susp -> norm [label="chSysEnable()"];
    disab -> norm [label="chSysEnable()"];
    disab -> susp [label="chSysSuspend()"];
    slock -> ilock [label="Context Switch", dir="both"];
    norm -> sri [label="Regular IRQ", style="dotted"];
    sri -> norm [label="Regular IRQ return"];
    sri -> ilock [label="chSysLockFromISR()", constraint=false];
    ilock -> sri [label="chSysUnlockFromISR()"];
    norm -> sleep [label="Idle Thread"];
    sleep -> sri [label="Regular IRQ", style="dotted"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 * The <b>SFI</b>, <b>Halted</b> and <b>SNMI</b> states are not shown above
 * because they are reachable from most states:
 *
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.75", height="0.75"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    any1 [label="Any State\nexcept *"];
    sfi [label="SFI"];
    any1 -> sfi [style="dotted", label="Fast IRQ"];
    sfi -> any1 [label="Fast IRQ return"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.75", height="0.75"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    any2 [label="Any State"];
    halt [label="Halted"];
    snmi [label="SNMI"];
    any2 -> halt [label="chSysHalt()"];
    any2 -> snmi [label="Synchronous NMI"];
    any2 -> snmi [label="Asynchronous NMI", style="dotted"];
    snmi -> any2 [label="NMI return"];
    halt -> snmi [label="Asynchronous NMI", style="dotted"];
    snmi -> halt [label="NMI return"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @attention * except: <b>Init</b>, <b>Halted</b>, <b>SNMI</b>, <b>Disabled</b>.
 *
 * @section interrupt_classes Interrupt Classes
 * There are three logical interrupt classes:
 * - <b>Regular Interrupts</b>. Maskable interrupt sources that cannot
 *   preempt the kernel lock zones and can therefore invoke I-Class APIs
 *   from within their handlers, after entering the I-Locked state.
 * - <b>Fast Interrupts</b>. Maskable interrupt sources able to preempt the
 *   kernel lock zones, they have a lower latency and are less subject to
 *   jitter. Fast interrupt handlers can only invoke X-Class APIs, they can
 *   however trigger a lower priority regular interrupt where the rest of the
 *   work can be done. Fast interrupts are not supported on all the
 *   architectures.
 * - <b>Non-Maskable Interrupts</b>. Interrupt sources out of the operating
 *   system control, with the lowest latency. Their handlers cannot invoke
 *   any API, X-Class included. Not supported on all the architectures.
 * .
 * The mapping of the logical classes on the physical interrupt priorities is
 * port-dependent, see the documentation of the port in use.
 *
 * @subsection irq_handlers Interrupt Handlers
 * Regular interrupt handlers are declared with @p CH_IRQ_HANDLER() and
 * bracketed by @p CH_IRQ_PROLOGUE() and @p CH_IRQ_EPILOGUE(), which also
 * give the kernel a chance to reschedule on exit. I-Class APIs are invoked
 * within a lock zone:
 * @code{.c}
CH_IRQ_HANDLER(myIRQHandler) {

  CH_IRQ_PROLOGUE();

  /* Interrupt handling code, it can be preempted on architectures
     supporting nested interrupts.*/

  chSysLockFromISR();
  /* Invocation of I-Class APIs, this zone is never preempted by regular
     interrupts.*/
  chSemSignalI(&mysem);
  chSysUnlockFromISR();

  /* More interrupt handling code.*/

  CH_IRQ_EPILOGUE();
}
 * @endcode
 * Fast interrupt handlers are declared with @p CH_FAST_IRQ_HANDLER(), they
 * have no prologue or epilogue and only X-Class APIs can be invoked:
 * @code{.c}
CH_FAST_IRQ_HANDLER(myFastIRQHandler) {

  /* Fast interrupt handling code, no lock zones and no APIs except X-Class
     ones.*/
}
 * @endcode
 * Callbacks invoked by the kernel from interrupt context, for example the
 * virtual timers callbacks, and the HAL driver callbacks follow the same
 * rule: they run outside the lock zone and must use chSysLockFromISR() and
 * chSysUnlockFromISR() around I-Class APIs, unless their documentation
 * states otherwise.
 *
 * @section thread_concepts Threads
 * A thread is an independent flow of execution: a function with its own
 * stack, processor context and state. Threads are represented by
 * @p thread_t structures and exist in the following forms:
 * - <b>Created</b> threads, the @p thread_t structure is allocated inside
 *   the thread working area, see @ref warea. Functions: chThdCreateStatic(),
 *   chThdCreate(), chThdCreateSuspended(), chThdCreateFromHeap(),
 *   chThdCreateFromMemoryPool().
 * - <b>Spawned</b> threads, the @p thread_t structure is provided
 *   separately from the stack. Functions: chThdSpawnRunning(),
 *   chThdSpawnSuspended(), chThdSpawnFromHeap(),
 *   chThdSpawnFromMemoryPool().
 * .
 * A thread terminates by returning from its function or by invoking
 * chThdExit(), another thread can wait for its termination and retrieve its
 * exit code using chThdWait(). The spawn functions and the dynamic threads
 * are RT only, NIL threads are declared in a static threads table or created
 * with chThdCreate().
 *
 * @subsection thread_states Thread States
 * The following diagram shows the main thread state transitions in RT:
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.9", height="0.9"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    start [label="Start", style="bold"];
    wtstart [label="WTSTART"];
    ready [label="READY"];
    current [label="CURRENT"];
    suspended [label="SUSPENDED"];
    sleeping [label="SLEEPING"];
    waiting [label="Waiting\non object"];
    final [label="FINAL", style="bold"];
    start -> wtstart [label="create or spawn\nsuspended"];
    start -> ready [label="create or spawn"];
    wtstart -> ready [label="chThdStart()"];
    ready -> current [label="dispatch"];
    current -> ready [label="preemption\nor yield"];
    current -> sleeping [label="chThdSleep()"];
    sleeping -> ready [label="time elapsed"];
    current -> suspended [label="chThdSuspendS()"];
    suspended -> ready [label="chThdResumeI()"];
    current -> waiting [label="wait"];
    waiting -> ready [label="signal, reset\nor timeout"];
    current -> final [label="chThdExit()"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.9", height="0.9"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    start [label="Start", style="bold"];
    wtstart [label="WTSTART"];
    ready [label="READY"];
    current [label="CURRENT"];
    suspended [label="SUSPENDED"];
    sleeping [label="SLEEPING"];
    waiting [label="Waiting\non object"];
    final [label="FINAL", style="bold"];
    start -> wtstart [label="create or spawn\nsuspended"];
    start -> ready [label="create or spawn"];
    wtstart -> ready [label="chThdStart()"];
    ready -> current [label="dispatch"];
    current -> ready [label="preemption\nor yield"];
    current -> sleeping [label="chThdSleep()"];
    sleeping -> ready [label="time elapsed"];
    current -> suspended [label="chThdSuspendS()"];
    suspended -> ready [label="chThdResumeI()"];
    current -> waiting [label="wait"];
    waiting -> ready [label="signal, reset\nor timeout"];
    current -> final [label="chThdExit()"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 *
 * The states are defined in @p chschd.h as @p CH_STATE_xxx:
 * | State     | Meaning                                                     |
 * |-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
 * | READY     | Ready to run, in the ready list.                            |
 * | CURRENT   | Running.                                                    |
 * | WTSTART   | Created or spawned in suspended state, waiting for chThdStart(). |
 * | SUSPENDED | Suspended on a thread reference, see @ref object_concepts.  |
 * | QUEUED    | Waiting on a threads queue.                                 |
 * | WTSEM     | Waiting on a semaphore.                                     |
 * | WTMTX     | Waiting on a mutex.                                         |
 * | WTCOND    | Waiting on a condition variable.                            |
 * | SLEEPING  | Sleeping for a time interval or until an absolute time.     |
 * | WTEXIT    | Waiting for the termination of another thread.              |
 * | WTOREVT   | Waiting for any of a set of events.                         |
 * | WTANDEVT  | Waiting for all the events of a set.                        |
 * | SNDMSGQ   | Sending a message, queued on the receiver.                  |
 * | SNDMSG    | Legacy state, currently unused.                             |
 * | WTMSG     | Waiting for a message.                                      |
 * | FINAL     | Terminated.                                                 |
 *
 * NIL threads have a subset of these states, defined in @p ch.h as
 * @p NIL_STATE_xxx.
 *
 * @subsection warea Thread Working Area
 * Each thread has a working area holding its stack. The working area layout
 * depends on the thread creation function:
 * - The <b>create</b> functions take a working area declared with
 *   @p THD_WORKING_AREA(), whose size @p THD_WORKING_AREA_SIZE() includes the
 *   @p thread_t structure. The structure is placed at the top of the working
 *   area and the stack grows downward below it.
 * - The <b>spawn</b> functions take a stack area declared with
 *   @p THD_STACK(), whose size @p THD_STACK_SIZE() does not include the
 *   @p thread_t structure. The caller provides the structure, which can be
 *   in a different memory, for example a coherent memory on multi-core
 *   systems with non-coherent caches. The chThdSpawnFromHeap() and
 *   chThdSpawnFromMemoryPool() functions allocate it from a memory pool.
 * .
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=plaintext, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    subgraph cluster_create {
      label="Created thread";
      fontname=Helvetica;
      fontsize=9;
      style="rounded,dashed";
      color="#999999";
      wacreate [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td border="0" align="right">working area end</td><td port="tp" bgcolor="#e8e8e8">thread_t</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td>stack in use<br/>(grows downward)</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td port="ctx">saved context<br/>(while not running)</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td height="48">free stack</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0" align="right">working area base</td><td>guard area<br/>(port option)</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      wacreate:tp:e -> wacreate:ctx:e [xlabel="stack\npointer"];
      spacer [label="", width=0.6, style=invis];
      wacreate -> spacer [style=invis];
    }
    subgraph cluster_spawn {
      label="Spawned thread";
      fontname=Helvetica;
      fontsize=9;
      style="rounded,dashed";
      color="#999999";
      tdspawn [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td bgcolor="#e8e8e8">thread_t</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      waspawn [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td>stack in use<br/>(grows downward)</td><td border="0" align="left">stack end</td></tr>
          <tr><td port="ctx">saved context<br/>(while not running)</td><td border="0"></td></tr>
          <tr><td height="48">free stack</td><td border="0"></td></tr>
          <tr><td>guard area<br/>(port option)</td><td border="0" align="left">stack base</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      tdspawn -> waspawn:ctx:w [label="stack\npointer"];
    }
    spacer -> tdspawn [style=invis];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=plaintext, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    subgraph cluster_create {
      label="Created thread";
      fontname=Helvetica;
      fontsize=9;
      style="rounded,dashed";
      color="#999999";
      wacreate [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td border="0" align="right">working area end</td><td port="tp" bgcolor="#e8e8e8">thread_t</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td>stack in use<br/>(grows downward)</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td port="ctx">saved context<br/>(while not running)</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0"></td><td height="48">free stack</td></tr>
          <tr><td border="0" align="right">working area base</td><td>guard area<br/>(port option)</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      wacreate:tp:e -> wacreate:ctx:e [xlabel="stack\npointer"];
      spacer [label="", width=0.6, style=invis];
      wacreate -> spacer [style=invis];
    }
    subgraph cluster_spawn {
      label="Spawned thread";
      fontname=Helvetica;
      fontsize=9;
      style="rounded,dashed";
      color="#999999";
      tdspawn [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td bgcolor="#e8e8e8">thread_t</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      waspawn [label=<
        <table border="0" cellborder="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6">
          <tr><td>stack in use<br/>(grows downward)</td><td border="0" align="left">stack end</td></tr>
          <tr><td port="ctx">saved context<br/>(while not running)</td><td border="0"></td></tr>
          <tr><td height="48">free stack</td><td border="0"></td></tr>
          <tr><td>guard area<br/>(port option)</td><td border="0" align="left">stack base</td></tr>
        </table>>];
      tdspawn -> waspawn:ctx:w [label="stack\npointer"];
    }
    spacer -> tdspawn [style=invis];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 *
 * The parameter @p n of the size macros is the stack space required by the
 * thread code. The port adds the space for the saved context, the stack
 * frames of interrupts served while the thread is running and, on some
 * ports, a guard area at the stack base used to detect stack overflows.
 * The context is saved on the thread stack when the thread is switched
 * out.<br>
 * The option @p CH_DBG_FILL_THREADS fills the stack with a known pattern
 * at creation, in order to measure the stack usage, the option
 * @p CH_DBG_ENABLE_STACK_CHECK enables a port-dependent stack overflow
 * check.
 *
 * @subsection priority Priority Levels
 * In RT priorities are numerical values of type @p tprio_t, higher values
 * mean higher priorities:
 * | Constant     | Value | Meaning                                          |
 * |--------------|-------|--------------------------------------------------|
 * | @p IDLEPRIO  | 1     | Reserved to the idle thread.                     |
 * | @p LOWPRIO   | 2     | Lowest priority of user threads.                 |
 * | @p NORMALPRIO | 128  | Central priority of user threads, the main thread runs at this priority after chSysInit(). |
 * | @p HIGHPRIO  | 255   | Highest priority of user threads.                |
 *
 * It is advisable to assign priorities relative to @p NORMALPRIO, for
 * example <tt>NORMALPRIO + 4</tt>. Several threads can share the same
 * priority. A thread changes its own priority with chThdSetPriority(),
 * mutexes implement the priority inheritance and can raise the priority of
 * the owner thread temporarily.<br>
 * In NIL each thread has its own priority, specified in its descriptor: the
 * priorities range from 0, the highest, to <tt>CH_CFG_MAX_THREADS - 1</tt>
 * and two threads cannot have the same priority. The idle thread is
 * implicitly the lowest priority one.
 *
 * @subsection scheduling Scheduling
 * The scheduler always executes the ready thread with the highest
 * priority, the kernel is fully preemptive. In RT the ready threads wait in
 * a ready list ordered by priority, the running thread is not in the list.
 * Threads with the same priority are executed in a cooperative way unless
 * @p CH_CFG_TIME_QUANTUM is greater than zero, in which case the threads
 * with the same priority are executed in round-robin. The round-robin
 * requires the periodic tick mode, see @ref time_concepts.<br>
 * On multi-core systems each core runs its own scheduler with its own ready
 * list, see @ref instance_concepts.
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    fontname=Helvetica;
    fontsize=9;
    node [shape=square, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.6", height="0.5"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    subgraph cluster_running {
      label="Running Thread";
      penwidth=0;
      current [label="current\npointer", style="bold"];
      T4 [label="Tuser(4)\nprio=200"];
    }
    subgraph cluster_rlist {
      label="Threads Ready for Execution";
      penwidth=0;
      rh [label="ready list\nheader", style="bold"];
      Ti [label="Tidle\nprio=1"];
      T1 [label="Tuser(1)\nprio=64"];
      T2 [label="Tuser(2)\nprio=64"];
      Tm [label="Tmain\nprio=128"];
      T3 [label="Tuser(3)\nprio=160"];
    }
    current -> T4;
    rh -> T3 -> Tm -> T2 -> T1 -> Ti -> rh [label="next"];
    rh -> Ti -> T1 -> T2 -> Tm -> T3 -> rh [label="prev"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    fontname=Helvetica;
    fontsize=9;
    node [shape=square, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.6", height="0.5"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    subgraph cluster_running {
      label="Running Thread";
      penwidth=0;
      current [label="current\npointer", style="bold"];
      T4 [label="Tuser(4)\nprio=200"];
    }
    subgraph cluster_rlist {
      label="Threads Ready for Execution";
      penwidth=0;
      rh [label="ready list\nheader", style="bold"];
      Ti [label="Tidle\nprio=1"];
      T1 [label="Tuser(1)\nprio=64"];
      T2 [label="Tuser(2)\nprio=64"];
      Tm [label="Tmain\nprio=128"];
      T3 [label="Tuser(3)\nprio=160"];
    }
    current -> T4;
    rh -> T3 -> Tm -> T2 -> T1 -> Ti -> rh [label="next"];
    rh -> Ti -> T1 -> T2 -> Tm -> T3 -> rh [label="prev"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 *
 * In NIL the scheduler executes the ready thread with the lowest priority
 * number, there is no round-robin because priorities are unique.
 *
 * @section time_concepts System Time
 * The system time is a counter of type @p systime_t incremented at the
 * frequency @p CH_CFG_ST_FREQUENCY, its width is selected by
 * @p CH_CFG_ST_RESOLUTION. The counter wraps around, time values are
 * compared using chTimeDiffX() and chTimeIsInRangeX() rather than directly.
 * Time intervals are of type @p sysinterval_t and are specified using the
 * conversion macros, for example <tt>TIME_MS2I(100)</tt>.<br>
 * The system time is implemented in one of two modes:
 * - <b>Periodic tick</b>, when @p CH_CFG_ST_TIMEDELTA is zero. A periodic
 *   interrupt increments the counter.
 * - <b>Tick-less</b>, when @p CH_CFG_ST_TIMEDELTA is greater than zero. The
 *   counter is a free-running hardware timer and an alarm is programmed only
 *   for the next timed event, @p CH_CFG_ST_TIMEDELTA is the minimum interval
 *   that can be programmed. This mode reduces the interrupts and the power
 *   consumption, the round-robin is not supported.
 * .
 * Functions waiting with a timeout take a @p sysinterval_t parameter with
 * two special values: @p TIME_IMMEDIATE, the function does not wait and
 * returns immediately, and @p TIME_INFINITE, the function waits without a
 * timeout. A wait that times out returns @p MSG_TIMEOUT.<br>
 * RT also provides 64-bit monotonic time stamps of type @p systimestamp_t,
 * enabled by @p CH_CFG_USE_TIMESTAMP, and the virtual timers, one-shot or
 * continuous timers whose callbacks are invoked from the system timer
 * interrupt, see @ref irq_handlers.
 *
 * @section object_concepts Kernel Objects
 * Kernel objects, for example semaphores, mutexes and virtual timers, are
 * structures provided by the application, the kernel does not allocate
 * memory for them. The OS library provides memory allocators and an
 * objects factory for dynamic objects.
 * - Objects are initialized at runtime using their constructor, for example
 *   chSemObjectInit(), or statically using their initializer macro, for
 *   example @p SEMAPHORE_DECL().
 * - Objects that are no longer used can be disposed using their finalizer,
 *   for example chSemObjectDispose(). Disposing an object does not release
 *   memory, it checks that the object is in a state compatible with
 *   stopping its use. With @p CH_CFG_HARDENING_LEVEL greater than zero the
 *   object is also cleared, so that further uses fail early.
 * - Most wait functions return a message of type @p msg_t, @p MSG_OK on a
 *   normal wakeup, @p MSG_TIMEOUT when the timeout expired and
 *   @p MSG_RESET when the object was reset while waiting, other values are
 *   object-specific.
 * - A thread reference, of type @p thread_reference_t, allows a thread to
 *   suspend itself waiting for an event, chThdSuspendS() or
 *   chThdSuspendTimeoutS(), and another thread or an interrupt handler to
 *   resume it with a message, chThdResumeI(). It is a lightweight way to
 *   synchronize a thread with an interrupt handler.
 * .
 *
 * @section instance_concepts OS Instances and Multi-Core
 * In RT the kernel state of each core is an OS instance, of type
 * @p os_instance_t, with its own ready list, virtual timers list and idle
 * thread. A single-core system has one instance. Each thread belongs to the
 * instance specified when it is created and is executed only by the core
 * of that instance.<br>
 * The option @p CH_CFG_SMP_MODE selects how the instances interact:
 * - <b>Disabled</b>, decoupled multi-core: each instance is a separate
 *   system, threads of different instances do not share kernel objects.
 * - <b>Enabled</b>, SMP: threads of all the instances can interact using
 *   the same kernel objects. The kernel lock zone also excludes the other
 *   cores. On systems with non-coherent caches the shared objects must be
 *   in coherent memory.
 * .
 * Variables can be placed in specific memories using the memory classes
 * defined in @p chmem.h, for example in memory private to a core or in
 * coherent memory. On multi-core systems with non-coherent caches the
 * @p thread_t structures must be in coherent memory while each stack can be
 * in the cacheable memory of its core, the spawn functions allow this
 * placement, see @ref warea.
 *
 * @section error_detection Error Detection
 * The kernel provides several layers of error detection, selected in
 * @p chconf.h:
 * | Option                        | Detection                               |
 * |-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
 * | @p CH_DBG_ENABLE_CHECKS       | Checks on the API parameters, chDbgCheck(). |
 * | @p CH_DBG_ENABLE_ASSERTS      | Kernel internal consistency checks, chDbgAssert(). |
 * | @p CH_DBG_SYSTEM_STATE_CHECK  | API classes call protocol, see @ref state_checker. |
 * | @p CH_DBG_ENABLE_STACK_CHECK  | Port-dependent stack overflow checks.   |
 * | @p CH_CFG_HARDENING_LEVEL     | RT only, functional safety checks detecting corruption caused by hardware faults or other software, chSftAssert(). |
 * | @p CH_CFG_USE_RFCU            | RT only, runtime faults collection: faults that do not stop the system are recorded as flags, read with chRFCUGetAndClearFaultsI(). |
 *
 * The debug options detect programming errors, the safety checks detect
 * faults that can also happen in a correct application. A failed check
 * halts the system using chSysHalt(): the interrupts are disabled, the
 * reason string is recorded for the debugger, the hook
 * @p CH_CFG_SYSTEM_HALT_HOOK is invoked and the system stops. The reaction
 * to the safety checks can be changed with @p CH_CFG_SAFETY_CHECK_HOOK.
 */
